      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "start",
        "title": "Supper on a tray",
        "description": "This is the prologue's last scene and its breath: warm, funny and short. Wren brings the player in and stammers out his report (or the player tells it); the King pours tea and guesses how they take it (he's usually right, and if he isn't he's delighted to be wrong), and wants to hear everything: the crossroads, the bridge, goblins a mile from his gate, the corporal's arm, and above all whether the cushions are safe. Let the King be the King: kind, curious, remembering small things out loud, a little frightened under it when the goblins come up, and grateful. Holt calls the minutes to the ninth bell. The point of the scene is that the player meets the King and likes him (or doesn't), and that he likes them: enough that in the morning, when something terrible happens, it's them he'll send for. Don't foreshadow the morning.",
        "cast": {
          "king": "Characterise: up out of his armchair to shake the player's hand with crumbs on his waistcoat, the tea guessed, the cushions rejoiced over ('Garran, they saved the cushions!'), small kind questions about the road. When the goblins or the grain wagons come up, a glance at his writing table.",
          "holt": "Characterise: by the window with his watch open, calling the minutes to the ninth bell, fewer each time.",
          "tob": "Characterise: just inside the door, at attention, report delivered in one breath; blushes when the King asks after his mother's chest."
        },
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "after-the-assembly",
