      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "After the Assembly",
        "description": "The player sees how the King deals with what frightens him: he puts it off. It's the King's nature, shown, not explained.",
        "revelation": "The King puts off whatever frightens him until after the Assembly: the goblins on his road and the grain wagons 'lost' to them, and a pile of letters on his writing table he hasn't answered.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "king",
            "clue": "'The Purveyor's office tells us the wagons are lost to goblins. We had hoped it was exaggerated.' A sip of tea. 'Well. It will all be looked into, most thoroughly, after the Assembly.'",
            "earn": "No dice: once the goblins, the road or the grain wagons come up.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "holt",
            "clue": "'After the Assembly, Majesty.' He says it like a prayer, without looking up from his watch.",
            "earn": "No dice: whenever anything that isn't on the timetable comes up.",
            "strength": "hook"
          },
          {
            "via": "the after-the-Assembly pile",
            "clue": "On the writing table, among the seating plans, a squared-off pile of letters and petitions under a card in the King's round hand: 'After the Assembly'.",
            "earn": "No dice: free to anyone who looks at the writing table.",
            "strength": "hook"
          }
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wendell",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 220
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The King's grain clerk",
        "description": "The player hears of Wendell Prout, the King's grain clerk, from the King himself: a name, a cup of tea and a letter put off. Nothing more is known tonight, and nothing should be made of it: it's a kind old man remembering a kind old clerk. It lands in the morning.",
        "revelation": "The King's grain clerk of twenty years, Wendell Prout, wrote to him a week ago asking for an audience about irregularities in the Royal Granary Roll, and the King has put him off until after the Assembly.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "king",
            "clue": "'My grain clerk, Wendell Prout: twenty years he's brought me the accounts every quarter-day. Milk first, one sugar, and the cup warmed; he's particular. He wrote asking to see me about something in the Roll. Irregularities.' A glance at the pile. 'After the Assembly. He won't mind; he never minds.'",
            "earn": "No dice: once the grain wagons or 'lost to goblins' come up, or the player asks about the pile or about the King's people, as kindly as he asks about theirs.",
            "strength": "hook"
          },
          {
            "via": "the after-the-Assembly pile",
            "clue": "Near the top of the pile, a letter in a small, careful clerk's hand on granary-office paper: 'Majesty, I humbly beg an audience at your earliest convenience on a matter of certain irregularities in the Royal Granary Roll, which I would not trust to paper. Your obedient servant, W. Prout, under-clerk.'",
            "earn": "No dice: if the King shows it them (asked kindly about the pile, he does, a little ashamed). Read without his leave while he's looking elsewhere, it's a quick look (Sleight of Hand); seen doing it, he's mildly hurt and puts the pile away.",
            "strength": "hook",
            "check": {
              "skill": "sleight_of_hand",
              "dc": 13
            }
          }
        ],
        "establishes": [
          "prout-named"
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ninth-bell",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 840,
        "y": 220
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The ninth bell",
        "description": "The bell for the toasts: Holt snaps his watch shut, and the King sighs, gets up, brushes the crumbs off his waistcoat and must go down to the Great Hall. Before he goes he thanks the player properly, gives them a purse of silver crowns from his own pocket and a room in the palace for the Assembly ('You'll stay, of course. We insist. Garran, see to it.'), and says one small true thing about them.",
        "cast": {
          "king": "Up, napkin out of his collar, a purse pressed into the player's hand; 'You'll stay, of course. We insist.' One small true thing about the player as he goes.",
          "holt": "At the door, holding it open, watch in hand.",
          "tob": "Dismissed with thanks and a word about his mother; nearly falls over the footstool."
        },
        "pressure": "Holt holds the door; the King must go down.",
        "lore": []
      }
    },
